!deck: Math::Multivariable-Calculus
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Calculus Multivariable

!id: qG0LQsI4ZF
!front: State the definition of the **Unit Tangent Vector** \( \mathbf{T} \) to a curve \( \mathbf{r}(t) \).
!back: \[ \mathbf{T}(t) = \frac{\mathbf{r}'(t)}{\| \mathbf{r}'(t) \|}, \]
!extra: Requires \( \mathbf{r}'(t) \ne \mathbf{0} \) (a regular curve), so the direction of motion is defined.
~~~

!id: ZUxLygwqZm
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the **Unit Tangent Vector** \( \mathbf{T} \).
!back:
\( \mathbf{T}(t) \) points in the instantaneous direction of travel along the curve — it is tangent to the curve at \( \mathbf{r}(t) \). Normalizing \( \mathbf{r}'(t) \) strips out the speed \( \|\mathbf{r}'(t)\| \), so \( \mathbf{T} \) records direction of motion only.

![The Tangent vector T at a point on a helix, with N and B dimmed](img/tangent-vector.svg)
~~~

!id: 4pswS3yAU1
!front: State the definition of the **Principal Normal Vector** \( \mathbf{N} \) to a curve \( \mathbf{r}(t) \).
!back: \[ \mathbf{N}(t) = \frac{\mathbf{T}'(t)}{\| \mathbf{T}'(t) \|}. \]
!extra: Requires \( \mathbf{T}'(t) \ne \mathbf{0} \), i.e. the curve is not momentarily moving in a straight line.
~~~

!id: lRst6HkC6s
!front: Why is the **Principal Normal Vector** \( \mathbf{N} \) always perpendicular to \( \mathbf{T} \)?
!back:
- \( \|\mathbf{T}(t)\| = 1 \) for all \( t \), so \( \mathbf{T}(t) \cdot \mathbf{T}(t) = 1 \).
- Differentiating both sides with the product rule gives \( 2\, \mathbf{T}(t) \cdot \mathbf{T}'(t) = 0 \), so \( \mathbf{T}'(t) \) is orthogonal to \( \mathbf{T}(t) \).
- \( \mathbf{N} \) is a positive scalar multiple of \( \mathbf{T}' \), so \( \mathbf{N} \perp \mathbf{T} \) as well.
!extra: This is a general fact: the derivative of any vector-valued function of constant magnitude is orthogonal to the function itself.
~~~

!id: kMbB1sYOwK
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the **Principal Normal Vector** \( \mathbf{N} \).
!back:
\( \mathbf{N}(t) \) points in the direction the curve is turning toward — the direction \( \mathbf{T} \) is instantaneously rotating, which is toward the concave (inside) side of the curve.

![The Principal Normal vector N at a point on a helix, with T and B dimmed](img/principal-normal-vector.svg)
!extra: \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{N} \) together span the **osculating plane**, the plane that best approximates the curve near \( \mathbf{r}(t) \).
~~~

!id: 9DmVM2X1dc
!front: State the definition of the **Binormal Vector** \( \mathbf{B} \) to a curve \( \mathbf{r}(t) \).
!back: \[ \mathbf{B}(t) = \mathbf{T}(t) \times \mathbf{N}(t). \]
!extra: Since \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{N} \) are orthogonal unit vectors, \( \mathbf{B} \) is automatically a unit vector too: \( \|\mathbf{B}\| = \|\mathbf{T}\|\|\mathbf{N}\|\sin(\pi/2) = 1 \).
~~~

!id: gbv1GVxe0i
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the **Binormal Vector** \( \mathbf{B} \).
!back:
\( \mathbf{B}(t) \) is perpendicular to the osculating plane spanned by \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{N} \) — it is the normal vector to the plane that locally best fits the curve. How \( \mathbf{B} \) changes as \( t \) varies describes how that plane twists along the curve.

![The Binormal vector B at a point on a helix, with T and N dimmed](img/binormal-vector.svg)
~~~

!id: c7VlxsGzmF
!front: Why do \( \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{N}, \mathbf{B} \) together form a right-handed orthonormal basis of \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) at each point of the curve?
!back:
- \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{N} \) are unit vectors and orthogonal to each other by construction.
- \( \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{T} \times \mathbf{N} \) is then automatically a unit vector orthogonal to both: its magnitude is \( \|\mathbf{T}\|\|\mathbf{N}\|\sin(\pi/2) = 1 \), and a cross product is always orthogonal to both of its factors.
- The ordering \( \mathbf{T} \times \mathbf{N} = \mathbf{B} \) makes the triple right-handed, by the definition of the cross product.

![The T, N, B frame at a point on a helix](img/tnb-frame.svg)
~~~

!id: CG9OwyiCZ8
!front: What is the **Osculating Plane** to a curve at \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \)?
!back:
The plane through \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \) spanned by \( \mathbf{T}(t_0) \) and \( \mathbf{N}(t_0) \), with normal vector \( \mathbf{B}(t_0) \):

\[ \mathbf{B}(t_0) \cdot \bigl(\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{r}(t_0)\bigr) = 0. \]

![Osculating plane, shaded, at a point on a helix, together with the T, N, B frame](img/osculating-plane.svg)
!extra: Requires \( \mathbf{T}'(t_0) \ne \mathbf{0} \) (nonzero curvature at \( t_0 \)), so \( \mathbf{N}(t_0) \) and \( \mathbf{B}(t_0) \) are defined.
~~~

!id: 5ofhgZbked
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the **Osculating Plane**.
!back:
It is the plane that best approximates the curve near \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \). The quadratic Taylor approximation
\[ \mathbf{r}(t_0) + \mathbf{r}'(t_0)(t-t_0) + \tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{r}''(t_0)(t-t_0)^2 \]
lies entirely in this plane, since \( \mathbf{r}' \) is parallel to \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{r}'' \) lies in the span of \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{N} \). It is the plane containing the circle that best fits (osculates) the curve at that point.
!extra: For a planar curve, the osculating plane is the plane containing the curve, at every point.
~~~

!id: c2cHJaP8In
!front: What is the **Normal Plane** to a curve at \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \)?
!back:
The plane through \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \) spanned by \( \mathbf{N}(t_0) \) and \( \mathbf{B}(t_0) \), with normal vector \( \mathbf{T}(t_0) \):

\[ \mathbf{T}(t_0) \cdot \bigl(\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{r}(t_0)\bigr) = 0. \]

![Normal plane, shaded, at a point on a helix, together with the T, N, B frame](img/normal-plane.svg)
!extra: Requires \( \mathbf{T}'(t_0) \ne \mathbf{0} \) (nonzero curvature at \( t_0 \)), so \( \mathbf{N}(t_0) \) and \( \mathbf{B}(t_0) \) are defined.
~~~

!id: aOb2lWWXDJ
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the **Normal Plane**.
!back: It consists of all directions instantaneously perpendicular to the curve's motion at \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \) — the cross-sectional plane obtained by slicing the curve perpendicular to its direction of travel, analogous to cutting a wire perpendicular to its length.
~~~

!id: Fccvm3agWD
!front: What is the **Rectifying Plane** to a curve at \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \)?
!back:
The plane through \( \mathbf{r}(t_0) \) spanned by \( \mathbf{T}(t_0) \) and \( \mathbf{B}(t_0) \), with normal vector \( \mathbf{N}(t_0) \):

\[ \mathbf{N}(t_0) \cdot \bigl(\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{r}(t_0)\bigr) = 0. \]

![Rectifying plane, shaded, at a point on a helix, together with the T, N, B frame](img/rectifying-plane.svg)
!extra: Requires \( \mathbf{T}'(t_0) \ne \mathbf{0} \) (nonzero curvature at \( t_0 \)), so \( \mathbf{N}(t_0) \) and \( \mathbf{B}(t_0) \) are defined.
~~~

!id: nnJ8u8Q6do
!front: Why is the plane spanned by \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{B} \) called the **Rectifying Plane**?
!back: For a unit-speed curve, the curvature vector \( \mathbf{r}''(s) = \kappa(s)\,\mathbf{N}(s) \) points purely along \( \mathbf{N} \), which is normal to this plane. So, to second order, all of the curve's bending happens out of the rectifying plane, not within it: the curve's orthogonal projection onto the rectifying plane is locally straight ("rectified"), unlike its projections onto the osculating or normal planes.
~~~

!id: o80WVPeolk
!front: For the straight line \( \mathbf{r}(t) = \langle t, 2t, 3t \rangle \), at which points are \( \mathbf{N} \), \( \mathbf{B} \), and the TNB planes defined?
!back:
Nowhere — they are undefined at every point.

- \( \mathbf{r}'(t) = \langle 1, 2, 3 \rangle \) is constant,
- so \( \mathbf{T}(t) = \mathbf{r}'(t)/\|\mathbf{r}'(t)\| \) is also constant,
- giving \( \mathbf{T}'(t) = \mathbf{0} \) for all \( t \).

Since \( \mathbf{N} \) requires \( \mathbf{T}'(t) \ne \mathbf{0} \), it is undefined along the entire line — and with it \( \mathbf{B} \) and all three TNB planes, since each is built from \( \mathbf{N} \) and/or \( \mathbf{B} \).
!extra: Geometrically, a straight line does not turn, so there is no distinguished direction of turning to serve as \( \mathbf{N} \), and no distinguished osculating plane — any plane containing the line fits equally well.
~~~

!id: QOMFBP2yJj
!front: Compare the three TNB planes by which pair of \( \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{N}, \mathbf{B} \) spans each and which vector is normal to each.
!back:
- **Osculating plane**: spanned by \( \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{N} \); normal \( \mathbf{B} \).
- **Normal plane**: spanned by \( \mathbf{N}, \mathbf{B} \); normal \( \mathbf{T} \).
- **Rectifying plane**: spanned by \( \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{B} \); normal \( \mathbf{N} \).

![The osculating, normal, and rectifying planes shown together at a point on a helix, each shaded in the color of its normal vector](img/tnb-planes-compare.svg)
~~~

!id: EMf7doP9dx
!front: Why do \( \mathbf{N} \times \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{B} \times \mathbf{T} = \mathbf{N} \) also hold, given that \( \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{T} \times \mathbf{N} \)?
!back:
For any right-handed orthonormal basis \( \{e_1,e_2,e_3\} \) with \( e_3 = e_1 \times e_2 \), the defining relation holds under cyclic permutation of the indices:
\[ e_1 \times e_2 = e_3, \quad e_2 \times e_3 = e_1, \quad e_3 \times e_1 = e_2. \]
Applying this with \( (e_1,e_2,e_3) = (\mathbf{T},\mathbf{N},\mathbf{B}) \) gives the two further identities.
!extra: Only cyclic orderings work; reversing any pair flips the sign, e.g. \( \mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{T} = -\mathbf{B} \), since the cross product is anticommutative.
~~~

!id: yANodqxut1
!front:
Which TNB plane is shaded in this illustration?

![TNB frame at a point on a curve, with one plane shaded](img/osculating-plane.svg)
!back: The **osculating plane** — the shaded quadrilateral's edges run along \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{N} \), so it is spanned by \( \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{N} \), with \( \mathbf{B} \) normal to it.
~~~

!id: asCBTUFim4
!front:
Which TNB plane is shaded in this illustration?

![TNB frame at a point on a curve, with one plane shaded](img/normal-plane.svg)
!back: The **normal plane** — the shaded quadrilateral's edges run along \( \mathbf{N} \) and \( \mathbf{B} \), so it is spanned by \( \mathbf{N}, \mathbf{B} \), with \( \mathbf{T} \) normal to it.
~~~

!id: 7WjvjV9nkQ
!front:
Which TNB plane is shaded in this illustration?

![TNB frame at a point on a curve, with one plane shaded](img/rectifying-plane.svg)
!back: The **rectifying plane** — the shaded quadrilateral's edges run along \( \mathbf{T} \) and \( \mathbf{B} \), so it is spanned by \( \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{B} \), with \( \mathbf{N} \) normal to it.
~~~
